<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Expect My Future to Be... – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Goals</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I Expect My Future to Be...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain abstract concepts, using simple analogies and native language when necessary. Concept: Aid in interpreting differences in communication.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain abstract career planning concepts, using simple analogies and visual aids (e.g., showing a road map image to illustrate "planning for a future").
            Can aid peers in understanding differences in career communication, using both languages and visuals (e.g., showing a chart of different careers and their study paths).
            Can interpret classmates' future goals using gestures, visuals, and simple sentences to aid understanding across different communication abilities (e.g., pointing to a picture of a civil engineer and saying, "A civil engineer builds roads and bridges").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows images of different career paths (e.g., doctor, engineer, artist, business owner) and a blank calendar/planner. Students brainstorm words related to 'future' and 'plans'. The teacher writes "future," "goals," and "plan" on the board. Students share one word they associate with "future."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the concept of a "School Planner" project. The teacher models explaining a simple daily task from a hypothetical planner, using modals of necessity and future time expressions. For example: "Tomorrow, I *have to* finish grading essays. I *must* prepare for Lesson 5. In the afternoon, I *will be attending* a meeting." The teacher demonstrates clarifying a term like "deadline" using a simple analogy: "A deadline is like the finish line in a race; you *have to* reach it before time runs out." The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of future tense structures and modals, focusing on /θ/ and /s/ minimal pairs (e.g., "math" vs. "mass" when discussing subjects). </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'You *must* study for the test,' does it mean it's optional or very important?" (Expected answer: Very important)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'plan' mean? Can you show me with a gesture?" (Expected answer: To prepare something in advance, students might make a sequence gesture).</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated handout showing different sections of a "Future Goals Planner" template (e.g., "My Weekly Schedule," "Monthly Goals," "Career Vision Board"). Each section has short, clear instructions on what to include, using target vocabulary (e.g., "Write your *goals* for the month," "What *career* do you *plan* for?").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in collaborative goal-setting discussions for future academic or career planning, offering suggestions and feedback.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main idea of this planner template?" "Why is it *important* to have a vision for your *future*?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match sections of the planner (e.g., "Monthly Goals") to their purpose (e.g., "To *set* specific targets you *want to achieve*").</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Explaining abstract concepts (e.g., "career vision," "long-term development") using simple analogies; aiding in interpreting differences in communication about future plans. Grammar: Modals of necessity ("have to," "must"), WH-questions for goals, Future continuous. Vocabulary: *future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development; plan, achieve, prepare, set, have to; ambitious, important, future-oriented; if, when; for, in; ambitiously, carefully.*</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice (Gap-fill):</b> Students complete sentences with appropriate modals or future continuous verbs based on context. Example: "I ___ (study) for my math test tomorrow." "You ___ (finish) your homework before playing." The teacher includes a focus on /θ/ and /s/ pronunciation.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled practice (Sentence Expansion):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A states a personal future goal (e.g., "I *want to* be a pilot."). Student B asks a WH-question to get more detail (e.g., "What *do you have to do* to *achieve* that *goal*?"). Student A responds, incorporating future continuous and relevant vocabulary (e.g., "I *will be studying* very *carefully* in high school if I *plan* to *achieve* that *career* *opportunity*."). The teacher monitors pronunciation of target structures.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Concept Clarification):</b> Students receive cards with abstract concepts related to future planning (e.g., "time management," "personal development," "career opportunity"). In pairs, Student A picks a card and tries to explain the concept using simple English words, gestures, or a simple analogy. Student B acts as the listener and then rephrases or asks a clarifying question. If necessary, Student A can use a simple native language equivalent for one or two key words to *mediate* understanding, then translate back to English. Example: "Time management is like being a director of a movie; you *must* organize everything so it runs *smoothly*."</li>
            </ol></p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student brings their drafted paper-based academic planner (the 21st Century Skills Project). In turns, one student acts as the "presenter" and explains a specific section of their planner (e.g., "My Goals for the Next Semester" or "My Weekly Study Schedule") to their group members. They explain *why* these *goals* are *important* and *what they have to do* to *achieve* them. Group members listen and, if they don't understand a goal or a task, they *mediate* by asking clarifying WH-questions (e.g., "What *will you be doing* on Tuesdays?", "When *do you plan to prepare* for that *opportunity*?") or requesting simpler explanations/analogies. The presenter *must* then rephrase or explain using simpler language, gestures, or visual aids (pointing to their planner) to ensure understanding across the group. They also offer encouragement to each other: "You *have to* work hard to *achieve* your *goals*!"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually explain one "short-term goal" from their School Planner to the teacher or a peer, articulating the steps they *have to* take. Then, they will select one classmate's goal and, using simple sentences and a visual aid (gesturing or pointing), interpret or summarize that goal and its required *development* steps. For example: "Maria's *goal* is to improve her *math* grade. She *has to* study every day after school."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of future tense structures and modals (e.g., "I *will study* hard," "You *have to* be ready").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of target vocabulary (*future, goals, plan, career, achieve, opportunity*).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining abstract concepts or interpreting peer's *goals* using simple analogies, visuals, or gestures.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of "have to" / "must" and WH-questions when discussing *plans*.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to *aid* understanding across communication abilities.</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you used to explain your *future plans* today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you help a classmate understand their *goal* better today?"</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel explaining your *future goals* to others in English now?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about helping a classmate understand their *plans* today, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The mediation activities were highly engaging, especially when students had to use analogies and visual aids for their "School Planner." Most students successfully used modals of necessity and future continuous to discuss their *goals* and *plans*. Some found it challenging to explain abstract concepts like "personal *development*" without reverting to too much native language, requiring a greater focus on providing English analogies. I need to prepare more varied visual prompts for abstract concepts in future mediation tasks. The pronunciation of /θ/ and /s/ was addressed, but requires ongoing reinforcement.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "I Expect My Future to Be...". Students have presented their "School Planners" and mediated future *goals*. The next unit will build on these planning skills, perhaps focusing on "My Community and the *Future*" to apply their future-oriented thinking to a broader context, encouraging them to continue setting *ambitious goals* and having a clear *vision* for their *development*.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>